  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1105 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nA large 597 dam upper ridge dominates the southern states this\nevening. This feature will hold strong on Wednesday with highs in\nthe mid 90s to low 100s. Southerly winds will continue to feed in\nhumid air with dewpoints in the lower 70s across much of the\narea. A Heat Advisory is in effect from 11 am to 8 pm on\nWednesday for most of the Mid-South except northeast Mississippi\nand southeast West Tennessee where dewpoints and temperatures\nwill be slightly lower.\n\nThe upper ridge will weaken some on Thursday as a shortwave moves\nthrough the Great Lakes and pushes a cold front toward the\nregion. Before any associated rain arrives, temperatures will\nhave a chance to climb well into the 90s, and another Heat\nAdvisory will likely be needed on Thursday. With the upper ridge\nin place, the front will weaken as it struggles to push south. As\na result of the weakening system and the general upper ridging\nacross the region, expect QPF amounts to underwhelm. The LREF\ndepicts a 20 to 50 percent chance of one quarter inch or more. A\nfew strong storms could occur mainly later Thursday along the\nKentucky and Missouri borders, but the lack of significant shear\nwill limit any severe potential.\n\nThe upper ridge will re-strengthen during the weekend into early\nnext week, and temperatures will remain well above normal. We\ncannot rule out the possibility of more Heat Advisories over at\nleast part of the area. There is a low chance of diurnal\nthunderstorms each day as well. The medium range continues to\nlook hot with the stubborn upper ridge prevailing over the region\nand the 8-14 day temperatures remaining well above normal.",
